WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.110
In Apucarana, Brazil,

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:05.401
a pothole or a broken streetlight usually means a

00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:06.676
phone call, a form,

00:00:06.676 --> 00:00:09.564
and guessing which department is in charge.

00:00:09.564 --> 00:00:11.930
Fiscaliz.ai removes the guessing.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.373
A citizen just sends a message on Telegram.

00:00:15.373 --> 00:00:17.887
No app to install, no sign-up, no form.

00:00:17.887 --> 00:00:19.821
Here we use the web simulator,

00:00:19.821 --> 00:00:21.756
which runs the exact same bot.

00:00:21.756 --> 00:00:25.710
They describe the problem in their own words, typo and all.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.863
Claude Haiku extracts the problem,

00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:29.365
the category and the time.

00:00:29.365 --> 00:00:32.483
The street is matched against the census address file,

00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:34.735
misspellings included, in milliseconds.

00:00:34.735 --> 00:00:36.872
The bot only asks for what's missing,

00:00:36.872 --> 00:00:38.870
then shows a summary to confirm.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.893
One tap, and it's registered,

00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:42.597
routed to the right department,

00:00:42.597 --> 00:00:44.230
and live on the public map.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.872
Citizens can attach a photo.

00:00:46.872 --> 00:00:48.708
Before anything is published,

00:00:48.708 --> 00:00:51.746
a YOLO model blurs every face and license plate,

00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:53.518
and the original is deleted.

00:00:53.518 --> 00:00:56.010
Only the anonymized copy goes public.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.256
When someone else reports the same problem, in different

00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:08.084
words, there's no duplicate.

00:01:08.084 --> 00:01:10.630
Nearby open occurrences are candidates,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.242
and the AI confirms it's the same issue.

00:01:13.242 --> 00:01:14.874
The appeal grows instead:

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.570
now two people are asking for this fix.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.032
Guardrails come first.

00:01:20.032 --> 00:01:23.612
An emergency is sent straight to the emergency numbers,

00:01:23.612 --> 00:01:25.044
and nothing is stored.

00:01:25.044 --> 00:01:27.712
A water leak goes to the utility company.

00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:29.405
The AI only fills in data;

00:01:29.405 --> 00:01:32.008
a tested state machine decides the flow.

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:33.180
In our evaluation,

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.198
it got 53 of 54 messages right,

00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:37.170
and missed zero emergencies.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.061
The map is public and real-time:

00:01:40.061 --> 00:01:42.444
new reports appear without reloading.

00:01:42.444 --> 00:01:44.441
Each category has its own icon,

00:01:44.441 --> 00:01:48.047
and you can search and filter by category or department,

00:01:48.047 --> 00:01:49.207
right in the link.

00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:52.770
The same interface works on desktop and on the phone.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.292
For City Hall,

00:01:54.292 --> 00:01:56.523
each department gets its own queue,

00:01:56.523 --> 00:01:59.009
ranked by how many people are affected.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:02.388
The reliability page shows the automation in numbers,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.470
and the open data downloads as GeoJSON or CSV.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.915
After the repair,

00:02:10.915 --> 00:02:13.444
the bot asks the people who reported it whether

00:02:13.444 --> 00:02:14.897
the problem is really gone.

00:02:14.897 --> 00:02:17.533
Their answers close the occurrence, or reopen it.

00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:19.190
The citizen closes the loop.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.612
Fiscaliz.ai. You point it out.

00:02:24.612 --> 00:02:26.150
The city fixes it.
